1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,409
Turn a bicycle wheel slowly and
follow the valve with your finger.

2
00:00:04,409 --> 00:00:07,413
It rises, it falls, it
swings left and right.

3
00:00:07,813 --> 00:00:12,258
At every moment it has a height above
the hub, and a distance to the side.

4
00:00:12,708 --> 00:00:16,976
Aarav wondered about this as he
lifted his bicycle to fix the chain.

5
00:00:17,326 --> 00:00:20,496
For any angle the wheel
turns, how high is the valve?

6
00:00:20,996 --> 00:00:25,030
Those two numbers, the height and
the sideways distance, have names.

7
00:00:25,030 --> 00:00:26,640
They are sine and cosine.

8
00:00:27,290 --> 00:00:29,801
Let's make it exact on the unit circle.

9
00:00:30,151 --> 00:00:32,403
Turn an angle x from the point A.

10
00:00:32,403 --> 00:00:36,699
The terminal side meets the circle
at a point P, with coordinates a and

11
00:00:36,699 --> 00:00:37,599
b.

12
00:00:37,487 --> 00:00:41,356
NCERT defines cos x as
a, the x coordinate of P.

13
00:00:41,356 --> 00:00:44,589
And sin x as b, the y coordinate of P.

14
00:00:44,989 --> 00:00:50,053
So cosine tells how far P is to the
side, and sine tells how high P is.

15
00:00:50,053 --> 00:00:54,623
On a wheel of radius one, sine is
exactly the valve's height above the

16
00:00:54,623 --> 00:00:55,523
hub.

17
00:00:55,798 --> 00:00:59,014
Now let x count up, and
watch both values change.

18
00:00:59,414 --> 00:01:01,091
At zero, P is at A.

19
00:01:01,091 --> 00:01:03,910
Cos zero is one, and sin zero is zero.

20
00:01:04,309 --> 00:01:06,566
At pi by two, P is at the top.

21
00:01:06,566 --> 00:01:08,614
Cos is zero, and sin is one.

22
00:01:09,014 --> 00:01:11,225
At pi, P is on the far left.

23
00:01:11,225 --> 00:01:13,734
Cos is minus one, and sin is zero.

24
00:01:14,134 --> 00:01:16,884
At three pi by two, P is at the bottom.

25
00:01:16,884 --> 00:01:19,283
Cos is zero, and sin is minus one.

26
00:01:19,283 --> 00:01:21,303
At two pi, P is back at A.

27
00:01:21,953 --> 00:01:25,901
So exactly where is sine zero,
and where is cosine zero?

28
00:01:25,901 --> 00:01:27,279
Three small steps.

29
00:01:27,629 --> 00:01:28,529
Step one.

30
00:01:28,460 --> 00:01:31,035
Sin x is zero when P has no height.

31
00:01:31,035 --> 00:01:34,979
Then P sits on the x axis:
at A, or at the far left.

32
00:01:35,729 --> 00:01:36,629
Step two.

33
00:01:36,621 --> 00:01:41,775
P lands there at zero, pi, two
pi, and so on, in both directions.

34
00:01:41,775 --> 00:01:46,342
So sin x is zero exactly when
x is n pi, for any integer n.

35
00:01:47,092 --> 00:01:48,106
Step three.

36
00:01:48,106 --> 00:01:53,258
Cos x is zero when P sits on the
y axis, at the top or the bottom.

37
00:01:53,258 --> 00:01:56,500
That happens at odd
multiples of pi by two.

38
00:01:57,250 --> 00:02:00,668
We write that as two n
plus one, times pi by two.

39
00:02:01,318 --> 00:02:02,428
Side by side.

40
00:02:02,428 --> 00:02:05,058
Cosine is sideways, the x coordinate.

41
00:02:05,058 --> 00:02:10,000
At sixty degrees, P is half a unit
to the right, so cos sixty degrees is

42
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:10,900
one half.

43
00:02:11,243 --> 00:02:13,530
Sine is height, the y coordinate.

44
00:02:13,530 --> 00:02:18,481
At thirty degrees, P is half a unit
up, so sin thirty degrees is one half.

45
00:02:19,081 --> 00:02:20,103
Now you try.

46
00:02:20,103 --> 00:02:23,734
At pi by two, which one
is zero: sine, or cosine?

47
00:02:23,734 --> 00:02:24,954
Pause and think.

48
00:02:28,155 --> 00:02:29,055
Cosine!

49
00:02:28,819 --> 00:02:33,241
At pi by two, P is at the very
top, with no sideways distance.

50
00:02:33,241 --> 00:02:35,889
Its height, sin pi by two, is one.

51
00:02:36,539 --> 00:02:39,255
Now the other four
trigonometric functions.

52
00:02:39,255 --> 00:02:41,891
Each one is built from sine and cosine.

53
00:02:42,241 --> 00:02:44,808
Cosecant x is one over sin x.

54
00:02:44,808 --> 00:02:47,254
Secant x is one over cos x.

55
00:02:48,004 --> 00:02:50,836
Tangent x is sin x over cos x.

56
00:02:50,836 --> 00:02:53,794
Cotangent x is cos x over sin x.

57
00:02:54,544 --> 00:02:57,101
Division by zero is never allowed.

58
00:02:57,101 --> 00:03:02,338
So tan and sec need cos x to be non
zero, and cot and cosec need sin x

59
00:03:02,338 --> 00:03:03,711
to be non zero.

60
00:03:04,361 --> 00:03:05,499
Remember this.

61
00:03:05,499 --> 00:03:10,635
On the unit circle, cos x is the x
coordinate of P, and sin x is the y

62
00:03:10,635 --> 00:03:11,550
coordinate.

63
00:03:12,250 --> 00:03:13,157
Quick one.

64
00:03:13,157 --> 00:03:15,314
Is tan of pi by two defined?

65
00:03:15,314 --> 00:03:16,597
Pause and think.

66
00:03:19,797 --> 00:03:20,697
No!

67
00:03:20,317 --> 00:03:24,184
Tan is sin over cos, and
cos pi by two is zero.

68
00:03:24,184 --> 00:03:26,276
You cannot divide by zero.

69
00:03:26,926 --> 00:03:28,789
Now, one worked example.

70
00:03:28,789 --> 00:03:31,474
Find all six functions at pi by four.

71
00:03:31,824 --> 00:03:32,746
Step one.

72
00:03:32,746 --> 00:03:38,054
Pi by four cuts the right angle in
half, so P lies on the line y equals

73
00:03:38,054 --> 00:03:38,954
x.

74
00:03:38,515 --> 00:03:39,854
So a equals b.

75
00:03:40,604 --> 00:03:41,504
Step two.

76
00:03:41,380 --> 00:03:45,349
P is on the unit circle, so a
squared plus b squared is one.

77
00:03:45,349 --> 00:03:48,976
Then two a squared is one,
and a is one over root two.

78
00:03:49,726 --> 00:03:50,729
Step three.

79
00:03:50,729 --> 00:03:55,535
So cos pi by four and sin pi by
four are both one over root two.

80
00:03:56,285 --> 00:03:57,190
Step four.

81
00:03:57,190 --> 00:03:59,126
Tan and cot are both one.

82
00:03:59,126 --> 00:04:01,488
Sec and cosec are both root two.

83
00:04:02,238 --> 00:04:03,704
Now, the exam trap.

84
00:04:04,054 --> 00:04:07,858
Many students read cosec
x as sine inverse of x.

85
00:04:08,258 --> 00:04:11,490
But cosec x is one over
sin x, a reciprocal.

86
00:04:11,490 --> 00:04:15,718
Sine inverse is a different
idea, which you meet in Class 12.

87
00:04:16,368 --> 00:04:17,921
Where is this asked?

88
00:04:17,921 --> 00:04:22,852
In the PUC board exams: find the
trigonometric functions at a given angle,

89
00:04:22,852 --> 00:04:25,438
or from a point on the unit circle.

90
00:04:26,038 --> 00:04:27,461
Exam level now.

91
00:04:27,461 --> 00:04:29,643
Find cot x plus cosec x.

92
00:04:29,643 --> 00:04:31,126
Pause and solve.

93
00:04:36,526 --> 00:04:38,006
The answer is A.

94
00:04:38,006 --> 00:04:39,359
Minus one half.

95
00:04:39,859 --> 00:04:43,590
P is the point minus three
fifths, minus four fifths.

96
00:04:43,590 --> 00:04:48,088
So cos x is minus three fifths,
and sin x is minus four fifths.

97
00:04:48,588 --> 00:04:50,329
Cot x is cos over sin.

98
00:04:50,329 --> 00:04:54,248
Minus three fifths over minus
four fifths is three quarters.

99
00:04:54,748 --> 00:04:58,940
Cosec x is one over sin x,
which is minus five quarters.

100
00:04:58,940 --> 00:05:02,665
Three quarters minus five
quarters is minus one half.

101
00:05:03,265 --> 00:05:04,726
Here is your recap.

102
00:05:04,726 --> 00:05:06,227
Screenshot this card.

103
00:05:09,427 --> 00:05:12,426
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

104
00:05:12,826 --> 00:05:13,726
One.

105
00:05:13,363 --> 00:05:15,492
What are cos pi and sin pi?

106
00:05:16,392 --> 00:05:17,292
Two.

107
00:05:16,965 --> 00:05:19,874
Why is sec of pi by two not defined?

108
00:05:20,774 --> 00:05:21,674
Three.

109
00:05:21,426 --> 00:05:25,011
P is the point zero point
six, zero point eight.

110
00:05:25,011 --> 00:05:26,285
What is tan x?

111
00:05:27,385 --> 00:05:31,779
Next time: the valve can be above
or below the hub, left or right.

112
00:05:31,779 --> 00:05:35,130
Which signs do sine and
cosine take in each quadrant?
